+## Decision card and recorded authority
+
+Use one visible card per user decision, **at most 600 Unicode code points**
+including its ID/title, spaces, line breaks and any link text and target.
+Measure the complete authored card before presenting it (for example, Python
+`len(card)` on the exact Unicode string); a 601-character card must be rewritten.
+Do not silently truncate it. Keep the full technical explanation in evidence,
+one link away, and retain that pointer in the card when details are needed.
+
+```text
+<USER-ID> — <plain-language title> · <pending / answered / prior authority>
+For you: <concrete consequence of the choice>.
+Recommend: <answer and why>. Options: <A and consequence>; <B and consequence>.
+Details: <evidence path or link, when needed>.
+```
+
+For an answered card, label the actual answer separately from the recommendation.
+For prior authority, state the recorded answer and the action it covers. Both
+still fit the same cap. Present at most three open decisions at a time; queue
+the rest explicitly. A details link is not a substitute for stating the user
+consequence in the card, and opening or receiving evidence is not approval.
+
+### Record before action
+
+The **agent judges** whether an utterance is a user decision and what its scope
+is; tools transport typed records only. Before acting on a new user decision,
+PMO uses the existing `perry-task ask` and `perry-task answer` writers (see
+[ask/answer instructions](../work/reference/subcommands.md#user-input-queue)).
+Use the ID returned by `ask`, never an invented ID. A pending question stays
+unanswered and its dependent action waits. If the user already gave an explicit
+answer in the conversation, create the ask and record that actual answer
+immediately, **before** the dependent action; do not ask it again merely to
+complete the record. A dispatched executor hands missing records to PMO and
+waits for them rather than writing a foreign or copied store.
+
+First check existing recorded authority. If it covers this action, cite and
+reuse its actual USER ID without re-asking or broadening its scope. A spec
+amendment states the change and cites the USER ID authorizing it; an implementing
+commit cites its actual authorizing USER ID in the commit message. If authority
+does not cover the amendment, obtain and record a new decision before changing
+the spec or implementing it. A citation alone does not establish scope.
+
+Never manufacture a user answer from elapsed time, ambiguous assent, an agent's
+choice, or an unread report. Clarify an ambiguous decision before dependent
+action. Preserve the user's original scope and all existing named-human and
+high-stakes requirements; the escape hatch above cannot discharge those gates.
+
+Reversible choices made under the autonomy guidance above remain explicitly
+**agent-decided**, with a reason and revisit trigger in the project's decision
+log. They are not user decisions and do not acquire a USER answer. If the user
+delegates a choice, record the actual delegation; label the resulting agent
+selection as agent-decided, not as the user's selection.
+
+Length is a deterministic check. Whether an answer is explicit, an authority
+covers an action, or a choice belongs to the agent is a semantic review against
+the conversation and records, not Python natural-language classification.
+
